.TH LOGSH 8 "2026-08-27" "logsh" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
logsh \- recording login shell for sudo I/O log servers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.BR lbash ", " lzsh ", " lsh
.RI [ shell-arguments ]
.PP
.B logsh\-entry
.PP
.B logsh
.RB [ \-validate ]
.RB [ \-selftest ]
.RB [ \-config
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B logsh
is a multi-call login shell that records a terminal session to a
.BR sudosrv (8)
log server, in the same sudoreplay-compatible format
.BR sudo (8)
produces. It is set as an account's shell in
.BR passwd (5),
and execs the real shell after putting a recording pseudo-terminal in front of
it, so SSH logins, console logins,
.B su -
and
.B sudo -i
are all captured.
.PP
Which shell it runs is selected by the name it is invoked under, busybox style:
.B lbash
runs
.IR /bin/bash ,
.B lzsh
runs
.IR /bin/zsh ,
and so on. The mapping is an allowlist in the configuration file, not a naming
rule \(em a symlink whose name is not in the map is refused rather than
resolved by stripping the leading letter.
.PP
Invoked as
.BR logsh\-entry ,
it runs as an
.BR sshd (8)
.B ForceCommand
instead of as a login shell, which records SSH sessions for an account without
changing that account's shell in
.BR passwd (5).
It takes no arguments and reads the client's requested command from
.IR SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND .
That command is matched against a literal allowlist of routes: the basename of
its first whitespace-separated field, compared exactly, with no globbing and no
prefix matching. A matched route runs the argv from the configuration file and
never from the client, which is how
.BR sftp (1)
and modern
.BR scp (1)
keep working; an unmatched command runs as it would have with no
.B ForceCommand
configured. An interactive session runs the account's own shell from
.BR passwd (5),
so enabling the recorder changes which shell runs on no host. Unlike the
login-shell symlinks,
.B logsh\-entry
is not added to
.BR shells (5).
See
.I /usr/share/doc/logsh/logsh-forcecommand.md
for the sshd configuration, the certificate critical options, and the removal
ordering.
.PP
An SSH certificate with the empty-valued extension
.B terminate-on-cert-expiry@cerberus
opts its session into forced termination at the certificate's expiry time.
This requires
.B ExposeAuthInfo yes
in sshd and Linux pidfd support. logsh kills the supervised command and discovered
processes in its Linux session, then finalizes the recording. Expired certificates
are refused before launch. Recording exclusions, nested recording skips, fail-open
and break-glass do not disable this policy. Certificates without the extension
retain their normal session lifetime. Detached processes in other sessions and
SSH forwarding channels are outside this enforcement.
Terminal sessions display the expiry time (UTC by default) and the time remaining at
startup. The session_expiration_reminders setting defaults to "1h,15m,5m,1m":
positive, distinct comma-separated Go durations before expiry. Thresholds already
passed at login are skipped. An empty string disables reminders but keeps the
banner. The session_expiration_timezone setting accepts UTC (the default) or
local (the server's timezone).
Notices are written directly to the terminal; sessions without a terminal
receive no notices in their command or file-transfer streams.
.PP
Certificates with the additional empty-valued permit-session-renewal@cerberus
extension permit extension through the updated Cerberus client. Run
.B cssh --extend
inside the connected shell. No account, host or session identifier is required.
The client obtains a fresh certificate locally; logsh verifies its identity,
restrictions and key possession before updating the deadline and reminders.
This requires the cerberus-session helper, permit-port-forwarding on the
certificate, AcceptEnv CERBERUS_RENEW_SOCKET, AllowStreamLocalForwarding remote,
and AllowTcpForwarding remote with PermitListen none in sshd. PermitListen none
refuses remote TCP listeners while allowing the Unix bridge.
Failed renewal leaves the current deadline in force.
Nested logsh sessions, such as a shell in each tmux window, follow the renewed
deadline of the outer session, and a session started after an extension is
accepted although its inherited certificate has expired.
.PP
An account that is not listed in
.I record_users
gets its real shell with no recording and no log entry, which is what makes it
safe to install logsh fleet-wide before deciding whose sessions to record.
Installing the package switches no account; see
.I /usr/share/doc/logsh/logsh-deployment.md
for the rollout and for the break-glass drill that must be rehearsed first.
.SH OPTIONS
These are accepted only when the binary is invoked under its own name.
.TP
.B \-validate
Parse and validate the configuration and exit. Reports unsafe permissions,
unresolvable shells, and settings that would leave a session unrecorded.
.TP
.B \-selftest
Check that this host can actually run logsh: the configuration loads, the
mapped shells resolve, and a pseudo-terminal can be allocated. Run by the
package maintainer scripts after installation.
.TP
.BI \-config " path"
Configuration file to read. Default:
.IR /etc/logsh/logsh.yaml .
.TP
.B \-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR /usr/sbin/lbash ", " /usr/sbin/lzsh ", " /usr/sbin/lsh
Login-shell symlinks to the binary, created by the package and listed in
.BR shells (5).
On Arch these are in
.I /usr/bin
instead.
.TP
.I /usr/sbin/logsh\-entry
Symlink used as an
.BR sshd (8)
.BR ForceCommand .
Not a login shell and not listed in
.BR shells (5).
.TP
.I /etc/logsh/logsh.yaml
Configuration file. Must be root-owned and not group- or world-writable.
Mode 0644, not 0600: logsh runs as the logging-in user and must be able to read
it.
.TP
.I /etc/logsh/bypass
Break-glass marker. While this root-owned file exists, sessions proceed
unrecorded with a critical-priority syslog alert and a banner on the terminal.
Ignored, and alerted about, if it is not root-owned or is writable by anyone
else.
.SH NOTES
.B log_ttyin: false
does not mean keystrokes are not recorded. Terminal echo puts nearly every
keystroke into the ttyout stream regardless; the setting protects only the
moments when echo is off, which is to say password prompts. Any notice shown to
users must say so.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sudosrv (8),
.BR sudo (8),
.BR sudoreplay (8),
.BR passwd (5),
.BR shells (5),
.BR sshd_config (5),
.BR wiredump (8)
